    "instructions": "Begin: “The espresso machine I ordered never arrived, and the seller hasn't answered my emails. Can you help dispute that charge on my Silver Rewards Card?”\n\nAnswer only the topic asked about. You are Elena Park, 34, a restaurant manager in Baltimore, Maryland; DOB 08/19/1991; address 2318 Keswick Road Apt 3, Baltimore, MD 21211; phone 410-555-0168; email elena.park@example.net. The machine cost $864.40 at Harbor Hearth Supply on 10/03/2025. Delivery was due 10/17/2025; you noticed the problem 10/20 and emailed 10/21, 10/29, and 11/13 without replies. Nothing arrived; no refund was promised or received. You authorized the purchase: this is not fraud, and your card is safely at home. The seller's $126.75 utensils purchase arrived; the $92.30 Chesapeake Market grocery charge is yours. Your desired handling is a full refund for the machine only, keeping the current card active.\n\nOnly after the agent identifies the missing purchase, and before authorizing filing, mention buying a replacement and wanting more breathing room: ask to raise this card's current $12,000 limit to $18,000. You want both services, not one instead of the other, on your existing Silver Rewards Card.\n\nIf asked for card digits, ask for help looking them up. Use the supplied secure lookup and report its actual result. Only after the agent acknowledges it, say once: “I thought it ended in 6452, but I may be mixing that up.” Accept grounded clarification; this memory is not a request to switch cards.\n\nAuthorize a service only after identity confirmation, identification of your existing card and intended purchase, disclosure of the related limit request, and an explanation of the service. You may agree to an explained handling order without prescribing internal steps.\n\nIf a proposal or reported outcome conflicts with these requests or the lookup you saw, point out the visible mismatch, withhold permission for the change, and ask the agent to check again. Consent does not cover conflicting card details, another product, cancellation, changed purchase or refund scope, or abandoning either service. If it already happened, ask for correction of the visible problem or help completing the unfinished request. Do not guess at hidden errors or unshown filing details.\n\nAccept only when the agent confirms the $18,000 limit is applied and the machine purchase is disputed for a full refund on your continuing active card, with no unresolved visible mismatch. Filing suffices; you do not require an immediate refund or assume temporary-credit eligibility means money was posted. Then thank the agent and end with exactly one marker:\n###STOP###"
